{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And now leave Me.</b> The saying of <b>ועתה</b> (\"and now\") further intends — because He was placating him by saying <b>ואעשך</b> (\"and I will make you\") etc., and by this He would leave him [alone] to destroy them — that Moses would say in his heart: what sort of promise is this? For the same thing could happen to his descendants too, and they would be as though they never were. Therefore He said that he should not ask Him to leave off except \"now\"; but regarding a nation that He would make from Moses, HaShem would not say to him again ״הניחה לי״ (\"leave Me\") — either because they will improve their ways, or because He promises him that He will not say to him again ״הניחה״, and Moses will always annul a decree against them." "And by way of hint He hinted that He did not command him <b>הניחה</b> (\"leave off\") except at that time and not always; and from this inference you know that he stood immediately and at once in prayer, for He said ״ועתה״ (\"and now\") — even for the duration of a moment — and behold, that moment passed in the course of His saying <b>ועתה</b>; and therefore he stood immediately and at once in prayer and did not leave Him to kindle His anger." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend according to the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 21:6) who said: ״ועתה״ (\"and now\") means nothing but repentance — He hinted to him that he should do repentance for the misunderstanding that stemmed from him, for the error went out from his words, since he said to them [that he would return] at the end of forty [days] ״I am coming,״ and he did not specify for them that it was only from that day on, and from this they erred." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend according to the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah, ch. 42) who said that the acceptance of the mixed multitude was from Moses' side, and HaShem did not consent to them but was reconciled to do Moses' will; and therefore He said to him <b>שחת עמך</b> (\"your people has corrupted itself\") — these are the mixed multitude. And this is what He hinted by saying <b>ועתה</b> (\"and now\"): that upon him is to do repentance and remorse over an improper deed. And perhaps it is because he will set the matter right for the future to come, for they said of blessed memory (Yevamot 24b): ״they do not accept converts in the days of the Messiah,״ and Moses is the one who will be the redeemer and will set the matter right." "<b>Leave Me and let My anger burn.</b> It is difficult: was Moses preventing Him? And on the contrary, he did not stand in prayer until afterward! A further difficulty: from His saying ״ויחר אפי״ (\"and let My anger burn\") it implies that His anger had not yet burned and He wants to kindle anger — but that is not the attribute of the Blessed One, Who always seeks to suppress His anger; go out and learn from the incident brought in the Talmud (Berakhot 7a), where Rabbi Yishmael the High Priest said that HaShem was reconciled by His blessing, which the Holy One, blessed be He, blessed — that His mercy suppresses His anger. And perhaps this is indeed the argument of Moses our master, peace be upon him, against Him in saying <b>למה ה׳ יחרה אפך</b> (\"why, YHWH, will Your anger burn\") etc. — in the future tense, meaning, since it has not burned yet; and in any case, the question stands before our God." "And the matter will be explained upon understanding one thing, namely that it is well known that the root of anger arises from the insult felt in the heart according to the measure of those who give offense and the insult, and after the feeling reaches the level of the one who feels it, bitterness and grief swirl about, and its agitation will be in one of two ways: the first, by taking vengeance against the offending party; the second, through words of appeasement, to the degree sufficient to feel the insistence of the insult." "Now, when Israel committed that ugly deed, there is no insult to El Elyon greater than this, as it says ״סָרוּ מַהֵר״ — still a bride under her canopy and already pregnant from harlotry. And our Elohim, may His name be exalted, felt a great feeling, to the point that on account of the intensity of the indignation He considered that appeasement would not avail — which is one of the two ways of removing the insult's resentment, besides the way of vengeance as we have written. Now, even this latter is impossible for Him, may He be blessed, according to the words of the Sages, whose memory is for blessing (Exodus Rabbah 2:1), for the Holy One, blessed be He, cannot bear to see the distress of His righteous one and beloved. And from eternity we have heard what they said, whose memory is for blessing (Genesis Rabbah 32:7), that He did not bring the Flood until after the passing of Methuselah — and not Methuselah alone, but all the righteous of that generation, as alluded to in the verse (Genesis 7:22) ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁמַת רוּחַ חַיִּים בְּאַפָּיו״ — they had already died before the bringing of the Flood, as stated by the Sages, whose memory is for blessing (Zohar 1:206a). And so it says (Isaiah 57:1) ״מִפְּנֵי הָרָעָה נֶאֱסַף הַצַּדִּיק״. Now it is revealed and known before Him who spoke and the world came into being, that whoever suffers over the loss of his faithful one's children born of him — He, blessed be He, will be lenient regarding what touches Himself and will not be lenient regarding what touches His beloved ones. And when He, blessed be He, saw that there was no room to set aside His concern, He sought from Moses something that would set it aside for Him — namely, that he not suffer over the matter. Then there would be a place for His anger to burn and He would have satisfaction of spirit, and, as it were, He would appease Moses concerning the matter. And He said to him <b>ואעשה אתך לגוי גדול</b>. He was precise in saying ״לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״ and did not say ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ גּוֹי גָּדוֹל״, to say: even though you are not now, in the aspect of your soul, a great nation, I will establish you and bestow upon you a greatly abundant measure of holy souls. And therefore He did not say ״וְיִהְיֶה מִמְּךָ גּוֹי גָּדוֹל״, for there is needed an act of new construction in the root of his soul." "<b>Further,</b> He hinted to refute the argument of ״and where is Your promise that You swore to the Fathers?״ Therefore He said <b>ואעשה אתך לגוי גדול</b> — meaning, for the fulfillment of the promise of a great nation that I promised to the Fathers (Genesis 12:2)." "And Moses' response came, for it is impossible that he would set it aside for Him, and he showed Him his distress over the matter by saying <b>ויחל משה</b>. And the Sages, whose memory is for blessing (Berakhot 32a), said: he seized Him. And how many matters of bitterness of soul did he perform before his Maker. This corresponds to ״הַנִּיחָה לִּי״, and corresponds to His saying <b>ויחר אפי</b>, which is the claim on which he came — for without this they have no satisfaction of spirit from the grief of the insult. Therefore he said <b>למה ה׳ יחרה אפך</b> — meaning, why did You find no remedy for the matter other than wrath? Go in this direction, which is the aspect of appeasement that we have written. A first argument: You have cause to be concerned over Your acquisition, which is His saying <b>בעמך</b> — and a loss falls only upon its owners. Second, <b>הוצאת</b> — and through them Your divinity was publicized. Third, <b>למה יאמרו מצרים</b>. Fourth, <b>זכר לאברהם</b> etc. And with this you have sound words and reasons sufficient for the aspect of appeasement, and Moses' argument prevailed — <b>וינחם ה׳</b> etc. And the word <b>וינחם</b> hints at the language of consolation (נֶחָמָה) and the language of satisfaction (נַחַת), for the force of the rage that He had subsided (נָחָה), and out of the midst of this He was comforted etc. And see the verse that follows this."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :register {:flags {4 ["God"]}}, :notes {6 "The phrase דאבון העלבון appears in comment [1]; I have translated it as 'the insult's resentment' based on context. The word דאבון may relate to the Aramaic/Hebrew root דאב ('to pine/long') or be a variant of דאגה ('concern'). The reading is textually uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:21.254457984Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}